DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  sseennddmmmmssgg()  system  call is an extension of sseennddmmssgg(2) that allows
       the caller to transmit multiple messages on a  socket  using  a  single
       system call.  (This has performance benefits for some applications.)

       The  _s_o_c_k_f_d argument is the file descriptor of the socket on which data
       is to be transmitted.

       The _m_s_g_v_e_c argument is a pointer to an  array  of  _m_m_s_g_h_d_r  structures.
       The size of this array is specified in _n.

       The _m_m_s_g_h_d_r structure is defined in _<_s_y_s_/_s_o_c_k_e_t_._h_> as:

           struct mmsghdr {
               struct msghdr msg_hdr;  /* Message header */
               unsigned int  msg_len;  /* Number of bytes transmitted */
           };

       The  _m_s_g___h_d_r  field  is a _m_s_g_h_d_r structure, as described in sseennddmmssgg(2).
       The _m_s_g___l_e_n field is used to return the number of bytes sent  from  the
       message  in  _m_s_g___h_d_r  (i.e., the same as the return value from a single
       sseennddmmssgg(2) call).

       The _f_l_a_g_s argument contains flags ORed together.   The  flags  are  the
       same as for sseennddmmssgg(2).

       A  blocking  sseennddmmmmssgg() call blocks until _n messages have been sent.  A
       nonblocking call sends as many messages as possible (up  to  the  limit
       specified by _n) and returns immediately.

       On return from sseennddmmmmssgg(), the _m_s_g___l_e_n fields of successive elements of
       _m_s_g_v_e_c  are updated to contain the number of bytes transmitted from the
       corresponding _m_s_g___h_d_r.  The return value of the call indicates the num‐
       ber of elements of _m_s_g_v_e_c that have been updated.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, sseennddmmmmssgg() returns the number of messages sent from _m_s_g_v_e_c;
       if this is less than _n, the caller can retry with a further  sseennddmmmmssgg()
       call to send the remaining messages.

       On error, -1 is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
       Errors  are  as  for sseennddmmssgg(2).  An error is returned only if no data‐
       grams could be sent.  See also BUGS.

